    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: '5000 Rummy',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '5000 Rummy is a rummy card game that involves melding, sets, runs. The game originated in North America and has become popular worldwide.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'North American rummy; many house variants (no two descriptions agree). Named for common target score 5000; also played to 1500, 2000, 2500, 10000. Related to 500 Rummy but with different draw and scoring.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '3–4 typically (2–8 with one or two decks). Deal and play clockwise.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '52 cards (3–4p); 104 (two packs) for 5–8p. Deal whole hands at once per player.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deck(s); score sheet to 5000 (or agreed target).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dealer gives each player their full hand before next player; first dealer by low card.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Target score variants (1500–10000): Groups may play to alternative targets such as 1500, 2000, 2200, 2500, 2800 or 10000 instead of 5000; rules of play stay the same but the game length and pacing change with the chosen goal.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Permanent wild cards and jokers: Some groups treat certain ranks (often twos) or added jokers as permanent wild cards worth high points, sometimes adjusting hand sizes or extra cards when a permanent wild appears as the upcard.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'George / 2500 Rummy (sets only): In the George / 2500 Rummy family, only sets of equal cards may be melded, runs are not allowed, and stricter conditions apply when taking multiple cards from the discard pile.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
